                        "language": "english",
                        "review": "Deadly Hunter VR is a simple first person defense game that puts me in the role of a hunter trying to clear out groups of orcs from different strongholds. The main idea is pretty straightforward. I have to learn what each enemy is vulnerable to, use the right traps and try to survive the attacks coming at me. Because it is made specifically for VR, I am actually moving my body to avoid axes and spears while trying to deal with enemies in front of me. I think that gives the basic defense gameplay a little more involvement than it would have with normal controls.\n\nThe trap system is probably the part I enjoy the most. There are 10 different traps to unlock, including things like steel traps, venom swamps and archer towers, and the different orcs have their own weaknesses. That gives me a reason to pay attention instead of simply using the same attack against everything. There are also more than 100 possible trap upgrade combinations, so there is a decent amount of room to improve my setup. I like when a defense game makes me think about which tool fits the enemy rather than just giving me stronger versions of everything.\n\nThere is also a reasonable amount of content for a small free VR game. The main game has more than 20 scenes and different challenges, with more than 10 enemy types including berserkers, spear users, shielded enemies and other creatures. Once I get through the regular stages, I can also try Elite and Endless challenges. That gives me something to work towards after learning the basic mechanics. The global leaderboards and 43 achievements add a little more incentive to replay stages and improve my results.\n\nThe VR combat itself can be quite fun when everything is working properly. Having to physically dodge attacks makes incoming enemies feel more threatening, and aiming for their heads gives me a clear target during fights. I like that the game is not just about standing still and waiting for enemies to reach me. I have to keep watching what is happening and react to attacks while also thinking about my traps. It is still a fairly simple game underneath everything, but VR makes that simplicity more active and gives the combat a bit more impact.\n\nThe biggest problem for me is that the game does not have enough depth to make all of these systems feel fully developed. The trap upgrades are useful, but the basic structure stays very similar throughout the experience. I am still defending an area, dealing with waves and trying to exploit enemy weaknesses. After I understand the main idea, there are not many completely new mechanics to keep surprising me. The visuals and environments are also fairly basic, which makes the game feel more like an older VR experiment than a fully polished modern VR title.\n\nI also think the free price is important when judging it. I am getting a proper collection of VR stages, several enemy types, traps, upgrades, Elite challenges and Endless challenges without having to pay anything. That makes the limited depth much easier for me to accept. I would not expect the same amount of polish or variety from this as I would from a much larger paid VR game. At the same time, the four hour estimate for the main experience means I am unlikely to spend a huge amount of time here once I have seen everything.\n\nOverall, Deadly Hunter VR is a decent little VR defense game that has a good central idea and enough content to justify trying it. I like physically dodging enemies, learning their weaknesses and choosing traps around those weaknesses, and the upgrade system gives me something to work towards. The repetitive structure, basic presentation and limited long term depth keep it from being one of my stronger VR games, but I still think the core gameplay works well for a free experience. For me, it is best enjoyed in shorter sessions when I want some straightforward VR combat without expecting a huge adventure.\n\nRating: 7/10\n",
